28. SCALABILITY
---------------
10 concurrent users: likely basic browsing workload if backend is warm and indexes/cache work, but no guarantee.
50 concurrent users: measure Render CPU/RAM, Mongo latency, image bandwidth, and cache misses.
100 concurrent users: possible thread, connection-pool, queue, and external-service pressure.
500+ concurrent users: likely requires shared cache, stronger database sizing, CDN strategy, background work, observability, and possibly horizontal scaling.

Actual capacity requires load testing.

29. ZERO-COST STRATEGY
----------------------
Preserve and measure:
1. Next search/detail caching.
2. Caffeine approved-PG cache.
3. Compound MongoDB indexes.
4. Pagination and maximum page size.
5. Next Image AVIF/WebP.
6. Lazy loading.
7. Compression.
8. Debounced suggestions.
9. Query explain plans.
10. Response DTO/projection.
11. Lightweight monitoring.
12. Staged load tests.

Do not add Redis or paid infrastructure before measuring the actual bottleneck.

30. END-TO-END FLOWS
--------------------
Search:
Browser -> Next /search -> backend client -> StudentController/SearchController -> StudentService/SearchService -> MongoDB -> PagedResponse -> PGCard.

PG detail:
Browser -> /pg/{id} -> getListing -> backend -> approved PG lookup -> owner lookup -> PGPublicResponseMapper -> PGDetailsResponse -> Next revalidation -> UI.

Owner create:
Owner -> authenticated request -> JWT filter -> PGController -> PGService -> authenticated owner -> verification/active checks -> ownerId assignment -> PENDING status -> image/database operations -> response.

Contact:
Browser -> CAPTCHA -> contact/report POST -> CAPTCHA verification -> honeypot -> rate limit -> validation/escaping -> MongoDB -> EmailService -> SMTP/Brevo -> response.

Admin:
Admin -> JWT -> ADMIN authorization -> AdminController -> AdminService -> status/ownership checks -> MongoDB -> notification/response.

