32. ARCHITECTURE DECISIONS
--------------------------
Next.js: selected for App Router, server/client rendering, and image optimization.
Spring Boot: selected for modular REST services, validation, and Spring Security.
MongoDB: document model suits PG/owner data; indexes are required for query performance.
JWT HttpOnly cookie: protects token from normal client-side JavaScript access but requires careful cookie/CORS configuration.
Cloudinary: external image/document storage and CDN-style delivery.
Caffeine: zero-additional-cost in-memory backend cache; not shared across instances.
Pagination: limits response size and database/application work.
Mongo indexes: support approval, city, gender, rent, owner lookup, and text search.
Public DTOs: detail mapper exists; public list DTO is recommended because raw PG entity responses remain exposed.

33. CODE QUALITY AUDIT
----------------------
GOOD:
- Module-oriented backend packages.
- Controllers/services/repositories separation.
- DTOs for important public/auth flows.
- Ownership checks near owner operations.
- Central endpoint constants.
- Shared frontend schemas.
- Security-focused tests.
- Infrastructure integrations separated.

NEEDS IMPROVEMENT:
- Introduce public listing DTO/projection.
- Align frontend/backend contracts.
- Reduce duplicate auth/session abstractions.
- Standardize formatting and configuration naming.
- Document deployment source of truth.

RISK:
- Exposed environment credentials require rotation.
- Refresh-token flow is unclear.
- Filter/image limits differ.
- README and implementation contain technology/port differences.

34. FINAL PROJECT MAP
---------------------
StudentPG
|-- Frontend: Next.js, React, TypeScript, Tailwind, RTK Query
|-- Backend: Spring Boot, Java, Spring Security, MongoDB
|-- Database: MongoDB collections for PGs, owners, admins, notifications, tokens, forms
|-- External services: Cloudinary, SMTP, Brevo fallback
|-- Authentication: JWT HttpOnly cookie, OWNER/ADMIN roles, BCrypt
|-- Deployment files: Docker, Docker Compose, Nginx, CI workflow
|-- Testing: JUnit/Spring tests, Vitest, TypeScript/build checks

Request lifecycle:
Browser -> Next.js -> API/proxy -> JWT/security -> Controller -> Service -> Cache/Repository -> MongoDB -> external service when needed -> DTO -> Next.js/RTK Query -> Browser.

35. FINAL CHEAT SHEET
---------------------
Project: StudentPG
Frontend: frontend/; Next.js + React + TypeScript
Backend: backend/; Spring Boot + Java
Database: MongoDB
Authentication: JWT HttpOnly access_token cookie, BCrypt, OWNER/ADMIN
Images: Cloudinary and Next Image
Email: SMTP with Brevo fallback
Caching: Caffeine approvedPgs plus Next revalidation (search 30s, detail 60s)
Main APIs: /api/auth, /api/owners, /api/owner/pgs, /api/student/pgs, /api/public, /api/search, /api/admin, /api/notifications, /api/contact, /api/report-issue
Main models: PG, Owner, Admin, Notification, RefreshToken, PGImage
Health: /actuator/health
Commands:
- cd frontend; npm install; npm run dev; npm run build; npm test; npx tsc --noEmit
- cd backend; .\mvnw.cmd spring-boot:run; .\mvnw.cmd compile; .\mvnw.cmd test
Security rules: never trust client ownerId; preserve role/ownership checks; never expose secrets/passwords/documents; preserve CAPTCHA/rate limits; never commit .env.
Performance rules: preserve pagination, indexes, caching, image optimization, compression, and DTO boundaries.

